1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,872
How does ecom get a certificate from the card zone without handing over its secret key?

2
00:00:04,922 --> 00:00:06,744
Start with the wrong way.

3
00:00:06,794 --> 00:00:11,256
One team makes a private key for the caller, and emails it across.

4
00:00:11,306 --> 00:00:15,264
Now one product's secret sits in another product's inbox.

5
00:00:15,314 --> 00:00:18,432
Whoever holds that key can pretend to be the caller.

6
00:00:18,482 --> 00:00:20,400
So the key must never cross.

7
00:00:20,450 --> 00:00:22,104
Think of a passport office.

8
00:00:22,154 --> 00:00:25,368
To get a passport, you send an application with your photo.

9
00:00:25,418 --> 00:00:27,600
The office checks it and stamps it.

10
00:00:27,650 --> 00:00:30,384
It never needs your face, only the photo.

11
00:00:30,434 --> 00:00:32,256
A caller does the same.

12
00:00:32,306 --> 00:00:36,144
First, it makes its private key inside its own Vault.

13
00:00:36,194 --> 00:00:38,472
The key stays there, and never leaves.

14
00:00:38,522 --> 00:00:41,712
From that key, the caller makes an application form.

15
00:00:41,762 --> 00:00:45,144
The form carries the key's public half and the caller's name.

16
00:00:45,194 --> 00:00:48,408
That form is called a certificate signing request.

17
00:00:48,458 --> 00:00:50,088
The caller sends only that.

18
00:00:50,138 --> 00:00:53,376
On the other side sits the card zone's passport office.

19
00:00:53,426 --> 00:00:58,296
It is the certificate authority: the one the card zone trusts to stamp certificates.

20
00:00:58,346 --> 00:01:00,168
It checks the form.

21
00:01:00,218 --> 00:01:02,040
Is it well made?

22
00:01:02,090 --> 00:01:03,912
Does it carry the right name?

23
00:01:03,962 --> 00:01:07,080
Is that name on the card zone's list of callers?

24
00:01:07,130 --> 00:01:09,720
If so, it signs it, for a limited time.

25
00:01:09,770 --> 00:01:14,208
The signed certificate is good only for calling in, never for serving.

26
00:01:14,258 --> 00:01:16,992
Then one folder goes back to the caller.

27
00:01:17,042 --> 00:01:18,816
Inside are four things.

28
00:01:18,866 --> 00:01:20,688
The signed certificate.

29
00:01:20,738 --> 00:01:25,104
The office's own seal, so the caller can recognise the real card zone.

30
00:01:25,154 --> 00:01:29,568
A secret for the login server, which travels by its own secure channel.

31
00:01:29,618 --> 00:01:31,128
And the addresses to call.

32
00:01:31,178 --> 00:01:32,904
The caller checks the folder.

33
00:01:32,954 --> 00:01:35,328
It stores everything in its own Vault.

34
00:01:35,378 --> 00:01:37,896
Then it deletes the loose secret file.

35
00:01:37,946 --> 00:01:44,424
From then on, every call it makes carries two proofs: the certificate, and a token from the login server.

36
00:01:44,474 --> 00:01:48,000
No private key ever crosses between the two products.

37
00:01:48,050 --> 00:01:53,304
And every certificate is watched, so a warning goes out well before it expires.

38
00:01:53,354 --> 00:01:55,176
Now watch it happen.

39
00:01:55,226 --> 00:01:59,568
There is no portal for this, so watch the steps the bank's two operators see.

40
00:01:59,618 --> 00:02:04,032
On the caller's side, the installer makes the key and writes out one small form.

41
00:02:04,082 --> 00:02:09,000
The operator carries that form, and only that form, to the card zone's operator.

42
00:02:09,050 --> 00:02:11,208
The card zone's operator signs it.

43
00:02:11,258 --> 00:02:17,424
If the name on it is not a listed caller, the signing stops with a clear refusal, and nothing is issued.

44
00:02:17,474 --> 00:02:23,424
If the name is listed, a folder appears, named for that caller, with the four things inside.

45
00:02:23,474 --> 00:02:25,536
The operator carries the folder back.

46
00:02:25,586 --> 00:02:29,160
The secret goes separately, by a secure channel.

47
00:02:29,210 --> 00:02:32,496
On the caller's side, the installer reads the folder.

48
00:02:32,546 --> 00:02:36,864
It writes it all into the caller's Vault, and removes the secret file from disk.

49
00:02:36,914 --> 00:02:40,704
The first call then goes through the mail slot, and the card zone answers.

50
00:02:40,754 --> 00:02:45,744
Months later, the team sees a warning that the certificate is close to its end date.

51
00:02:45,794 --> 00:02:49,848
They repeat the same steps, and the old certificate retires on time.

52
00:02:49,898 --> 00:02:52,392
Nobody is woken by a sudden outage.

53
00:02:52,442 --> 00:02:54,264
What does this protect?

54
00:02:54,314 --> 00:02:57,912
Without this exchange, one product would hold another's secret.

55
00:02:57,962 --> 00:03:00,408
A leak on either side would expose both.

56
00:03:00,458 --> 00:03:04,176
With it, each product keeps its own key, in its own Vault.

57
00:03:04,226 --> 00:03:07,320
The card zone decides who may call, and for how long.

58
00:03:07,370 --> 00:03:10,464
The caller never touches the card zone's keys.

59
00:03:10,514 --> 00:03:14,232
That keeps the two products truly separate, sharing nothing.

60
00:03:14,282 --> 00:03:20,976
It also keeps the bank's audit simple: every certificate has one owner, one signer, and one end date.

61
00:03:21,026 --> 00:03:22,848
Back to the passport.

62
00:03:22,898 --> 00:03:25,224
A stamped photo goes through the post.

63
00:03:25,274 --> 00:03:27,096
The face never does.

64
00:03:27,146 --> 00:03:31,464
Next: how the card zone stops a thief testing stolen cards.
